                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_campus_strong: `<strong>A deliberate lead-campus mix.</strong> Lille and Paris give you the faculty base and home market, while Suzhou adds a genuinely different market so the programme is relevant, not just replicated. That is portfolio thinking, not convenience.`,
                    coach_r1_campus_home:   `<strong>Three French campuses are easy to coordinate — and that's the problem.</strong> One market, one regime, little international reach. A 'global' MSc that never leaves France is hard to differentiate and hard to sell as a multi-campus flagship.`,
                    coach_r1_campus_spread: `<strong>Maximum reach, maximum complexity.</strong> Lille, Raleigh and Suzhou span three accreditation regimes and three time zones at once — coherence and faculty coordination get much harder before you've designed anything. Reach has to be financed and delivered.`,
                    coach_r1_campus_all:    `<strong>All five at once overstretches everything.</strong> You'll spread six core faculty across five sites, fragment the design, and blow the travel budget. The mandate was at least three campuses — start coherent, then scale.`,
                    coach_r1_binding_faculty: `<strong>Faculty is the right binding constraint.</strong> Six core faculty against a 9-FTE need at 88% utilization is what every other decision must bend around — you cannot staff a multi-campus programme on goodwill.`,
                    coach_r1_binding_sharp: `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> A defensible lead-campus mix AND naming faculty scarcity as the binding constraint — that is the lens that stops you designing a programme you cannot actually staff.`,
                    coach_r1_binding_partial: `<strong>Coherence and budget matter — but they're downstream of faculty.</strong> You can design a coherent, affordable programme on paper and still be unable to teach it. Six faculty against nine FTE is the constraint that shapes everything else.`,
                    coach_r1_binding_none:  `<strong>That's exactly how you get five programmes under one name.</strong> 'Each campus builds its own' is the default that drops coherence below the floor and fails accreditation. There is a binding constraint — start with faculty.`,
                    coach_r1_standalone:    `<strong>You're treating this as a standalone launch.</strong> The new MSc will pull students from your existing Master in Management — net new enrolment is far below the headline. A programme decision was made without looking at the portfolio.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>That's the integration-responsiveness balance.</strong> A shared core of roughly 55-70% of credits is coherent enough to carry the brand and satisfy accreditation, while the adaptable band keeps each campus market-relevant. Coherent without being identical.`,
                    coach_r2_lowish:        `<strong>A lean shared core leaves you relevant but exposed.</strong> Below 55% the common spine starts to thin — coherence climbs slowly and the accreditors will ask how a Raleigh and a Suzhou student earn the same degree. Push the core a little higher.`,
                    coach_r2_high:          `<strong>A heavy shared core is very coherent — but watch the market.</strong> Above 70% the locally adaptable band gets squeezed and enrolment in the distinct markets starts to soften. Coherence is necessary, not sufficient; leave room for relevance.`,
                    coach_r2_standardize:   `<strong>You mandated a near-identical curriculum everywhere.</strong> Coherence is excellent and the brand is solved — but the cases ignore the local markets, Suzhou enrolment collapses and the local director is in revolt. You solved the brand and broke the market. Where should local adaptation live?`,
                    coach_r2_fragment:      `<strong>That shared core is too thin to be one programme.</strong> Below 40% the campuses default to local design — you've got three programmes under one name, coherence stalls below the floor, and the EQUIS panel will ask what is actually shared.`,
                    coach_r2_spine_sharp:   `<strong>A shared assessment spine on a balanced core is exactly what accreditors want.</strong> Common rubrics and a capstone prove equivalence, while locally chosen cases keep it relevant. That is assessment equivalence without forcing one identical exam.`,
                    coach_r2_identical:     `<strong>Identical exams force equivalence the blunt way.</strong> The same paper everywhere is easy to defend on paper but ignores that a Suzhou cohort and a Raleigh cohort face different markets — it dents local relevance and enrolment.`,
                    coach_r2_localassess:   `<strong>Letting each campus set its own assessment breaks equivalence.</strong> Without a shared assessment model the accreditors cannot see how the degree is the same across sites — coherence drops and readiness is at risk.`,
                    coach_r2_underprice:    `<strong>You chased enrolment by cutting tuition and promising everything.</strong> Demand looks great at €18,000 — but the programme now loses money at scale and you still can't staff it. Enrolment is an outcome of a viable design, not a number to inflate.`,
                    coach_r3_sustainable:   `<strong>Sustainable staffing.</strong> By leaning on hiring, visiting faculty and online delivery you've kept core-faculty utilization below the 88% burnout line — a multi-campus programme cannot run on overloaded professors, and you've protected quality and retention.`,
                    coach_r3_tight:         `<strong>Utilization is right on the danger line.</strong> You're covering the campuses but your core faculty are at the 88% threshold — there's no slack for research or absence. A little more hiring or visiting cover would make this sustainable.`,
                    coach_r3_burnout:       `<strong>You covered the campuses by pushing utilization toward burnout.</strong> Two of your six sustainability professors are signalling they'll leave and teaching quality is slipping. A multi-campus programme cannot run on burnout — what is your hiring and visiting-faculty plan?`,
                    coach_r3_onlineheavy:   `<strong>Heavy online delivery covers the FTE cheaply but thins the experience.</strong> Too much shared online teaching undercuts the residential, cross-campus distinctiveness that justifies a premium MSc — and coherence of experience suffers.`,
                    coach_r3_hybrid:        `<strong>A hybrid International Week is cost-aware experience design.</strong> Virtual cross-campus weeks plus one residential core protect the signature experience at €260K — far less than the €480K full residential — leaving budget for the rest of the launch.`,
                    coach_r3_residential:   `<strong>The full residential week costs €480K — 20% of your entire budget.</strong> It's a wonderful experience, but it crowds out marketing and faculty hiring and pushes you toward the cap. Is there a hub-and-spoke or hybrid format that protects the signature for less?`,
                    coach_r3_virtual:       `<strong>A fully virtual week is cheap but loses the signature.</strong> The whole point of a multi-campus flagship is that cohorts converge — strip out the residential core entirely and you lose the differentiator that justifies the programme over a local one.`,
                    coach_r4_evidence:      `<strong>An evidence-led case is what the panel needs.</strong> Shared learning outcomes, assessment equivalence and faculty-sufficiency data per campus turn 'coherence' from a brand aspiration into the audited evidence EQUIS and AACSB require.`,
                    coach_r4_narrative:     `<strong>A good story isn't an accreditation case.</strong> The panel wants demonstrable learning-outcome assurance and equivalence across sites, not brand prose. Lead with the evidence and let the narrative support it.`,
                    coach_r4_thin:          `<strong>A headline number won't pass a readiness review.</strong> Submitting the coherence score alone invites the question you can't yet answer — how a Raleigh and a Suzhou student earn the same degree. Build the evidence per campus.`,
                    coach_r4_rush:          `<strong>Rushing all five campuses by year one outruns your faculty runway.</strong> You'll re-create the over-allocation problem and dilute coherence again. Scaling needs a hiring runway, not just ambition.`,
                    coach_r4_freeze:        `<strong>Never expanding caps the programme's value.</strong> Three campuses launch the flagship, but a multi-campus strategy that never reaches campuses four and five leaves reach and economics on the table. Phase it, don't freeze it.`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>That's a board-ready, honest case.</strong> A coherent core, sustainable staffing, an on-budget International Week and a staged roadmap — one coherent global programme you can actually deliver, not an inflated promise that collapses after launch.`,
                    coach_r4_overclaim:     `<strong>You boosted the pitch by promising 130 enrolled and every campus.</strong> Reach and enrolment have to be financed and delivered, not just sold. Over-promising to win approval is how launches miss break-even and lose accreditation credibility. Re-run the economics.`,
                    coach_r4_defer:         `<strong>Deferring the gaps to after launch is how readiness reviews fail.</strong> 'Approve now, fix later' breaks the public commitment when the gaps surface. The point of the 16-week gate is to be ready before you launch, not after.`,

